    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing).\n\n## What I did\n\n**Redesigned the identity key** (`OwnerIdentityKey.of`) to be the SHA-256 hex digest of `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)`, reusing the existing `Sha256.hex` helper. It now returns a 64-char lowercase hex string instead of the old plaintext `telephone|email|householdId`.\n\n**Added a `Soundex` utility** (`util/Soundex.java`) \u2014 the standard American Soundex algorithm (first letter + three digits, h/w non-separating, vowels resetting) \u2014 since none existed. It sits in its own single-responsibility class alongside the other `util` helpers.\n\n**Duplicate detection (409)** in `ClinicServiceImpl.rejectIdentityCollision` still compares the single identity key, still filters out soft-deleted owners (`isActive`), and the email-domain blocklist still runs first in `createOwner`. The telephone prefilter remains valid and efficient because equal keys imply equal telephones.\n\n**Soft match redesigned** \u2014 `flagPossibleDuplicate` now delegates to a new `findSoftMatchCandidates`, which flags a match when the identity key *differs* but `soundex(lastName)` and postcode match (lowest-id winner). This required a new `findByPostcode` repository method, implemented across the interface, the Spring Data (active profile), JPA, and JDBC implementations.\n\n**Removed the household-duplicate 409 path** \u2014 the `HouseholdDuplicateException` throw, the exception class, its `@ExceptionHandler`, its error-message constant, and the import are all gone. `householdMembers` is still computed and used only for `householdSize` and membership-level capping, which the spec leaves unchanged.\n\nNet behavioral effect matches the spec: two owners with the same last name and postcode but **different telephones** now derive different keys, so they're created as a soft match rather than rejected with 409; an exact identity repeat still gets a 409.",
